		IlluminanceI: {
			Title: Illuminance
			ContentsHeader: Contents

			Ch1: {
				Title: Ch. 1 | False Light
				Opening: My name is Laine L., and I ask a peculiar favour of you at the outset: believe me for forty pages.[p]I do not ask this because I am owed it. I ask because there is no other route to the thing I must show you, and because a man who tells you the end of a road before you have walked it has told you nothing except that he has been somewhere. I intend to take you along the way I went. You will find it a pleasant walk. I found it a pleasant walk. That is very nearly the whole of my complaint.
				Second: The word first. The optical men divide the light a surface receives from the light a surface gives off; they have a term for each; I took the wrong one. Illuminance is the falling light - the light that arrives from elsewhere and lands upon a thing that had nothing to do with making it.[p]I meant to say luminance. I meant the light we give off. I have been corrected on this point by better men than myself, in print, on three occasions, and I accepted the correction each time with the good grace of a man who does not intend to change anything.[p]I now believe the slip was the only unpremeditated truth of my career, and that I spent thirty years walking away from it in a straight line.
				Excerpt: I ask that you believe me for forty pages.
				AsideTitle: A note to the reader who skips
				AsideBody: Chapter the Fifth may be read alone and will make perfect sense, and you will have learned nothing whatsoever. I say this without irony. I have watched it happen.
				Closing: You will want to know what the Illuminance is before you are willing to walk.[p]It is the light we all bare, uncovered and unhideable, and it is the plainest thing in the world once you have seen it in a single face.[p]I saw it in a single face. I will not name her yet.[p]Turn the page, and I will show you how I came to look.
			}

			Ch2: {
				Title: Ch. 2 | Shapes
				Opening: I did not find the Illuminance. I want that on the record early, while I still have your goodwill.[p]What I found was a [gloss:Silhouette] - a shape with light behind it. I have spent the better part of my life describing the shape and calling it the light. I fear the confusion is common amongst our kind, and I put that modestly. As for the method - and I mean to set it down properly - the method is sound, and *that* is precisely the difficulty.
				Second: I came to it in the winter of my twenty-sixth year, in a parish I had gone to for an entirely different and much stupider reason. I had been keeping records of what I then called "civil temperature": the disposition of a village toward itself. Who is lent to without a note being written? Where do the quarrels stop? Which house is the sick man carried to, when there is a choice of houses? It was not a serious instrument. I was not yet a serious man.[p]But I kept it faithfully across eleven parishes, and in the eleventh the [gloss:Figures] went wrong, and stayed wrong, and were wrong in a shape.[p]There was something at the middle of that parish which I could not see, and I knew it was there the way you know a person has entered a room behind you: not by any evidence of the person, but by the change in the light on the wall you are already looking at.
				Third:
					'''
					On the objection I was given:
					It was put to me, at Leiden, that an instrument which must shine in order to read cannot be trusted to report on what it has shone at. I answered that this is true of the eye also. The room laughed. I took the laugh for an answer and carried it about with me for thirty years.
					'''
				Closing:
					'''
					Here, then, is the whole of my discovery, stated as plainly as I am able, and you will see too, that it is nothing:[p]There are people upon whom the light of others falls, and does not stop. It goes through, comes out altered, and lands on whoever is standing near.
					Most of us are surfaces. A very few are not. I could not measure that directly and I never have. What I measured was the darkness it made behind her, which I described lovingly and in great detail. The world thought it remarkable. They called it a discovery. I did not correct the world.
					After said events, I went to the eleventh parish in February to find out what was standing in the middle of my figures.[p]It was a woman hanging cloth, perfectly ordinary, and I have never in my life seen anything so bright.
					'''
			}

			Ch3: {
				Title: Ch. 3 | The Eleventh Parish
				Opening: I introduced myself quite poorly. I have introduced myself poorly perhaps four hundred times in my life and remember only this one, which should tell you something about the reliability of the account that follows.[p]I said that I was conducting a survey. She said that she was hanging washing, in the tone of a woman correcting an error of fact, and went on doing it. I stood in the cold and watched a stranger peg out sheets, and could not for the life of me have told you why I did not leave. I had my [gloss:Figures]. The figures were the reason I had come, and the figures were finished.[p]I stayed until the basket was empty. Then I asked whether I might come back tomorrow. She said the lane was public and she could hardly stop me, which I have since decided was the kindest thing anyone has ever said to me.
				Second: I will name her now. I said in the first chapter that I would not, and I find I cannot get through this chapter without it, which is its own kind of evidence.[p]Her name was Hesper - the evening star, the light that arrives at the end of the day from very far off, having had nothing whatever to do with the day it arrives at the end of. I did not choose her name. I have never been able to decide whether that makes it worse.[p]She was not a saint, and I will not make her one, since making her one is the whole of my offence in miniature. She was quick, impatient with fools, and rather vain about her hands. She thought my work was silly and said so. She had four hours a week that were her own; she spent two of them on me. I understood this to be a great concession, and never once asked what she had given up to make it.
				Third: I set out to be loved and I was successful, and I would like to describe how, because I was rather good at it and because the method should be preserved as a warning.[p]I brought her books. That was the whole of it. I brought her Locke; she thought him evasive, and said so in one sentence I have never satisfactorily answered. I brought better ones after that, and harder ones, and watched her go through them the way a fire goes through a dry season. Then I told her - I remember the words, I was pleased with them - that she was wasted here, in this provincial village.[p]*She did not agree with me. She did not argue either. I took the silence for assent, being a man who had never in his life encountered a silence that was not assent, and I said it again in September, and in October, and by the new year I had said it so often that she had begun to say it back to me, and I recorded this in my notebook as progress. It is the only page of that notebook I have burned. I am aware that burning it accomplished nothing. I burned it anyway, and then I sat and thought about what it would have cost me to have simply asked her, once, what she thought she was.*
				Excerpt: '''"I am not bright in the way you think. I am bright here."'''
				AsideTitle: On the keeping of notes
				AsideBody: A record taken openly changes the thing recorded, and every man in my profession knows it. We call this contamination and take pains against it. There is no term in our literature for a record taken quietly. I have looked. I would have used it.
				Fourth: She said it in February, a year to the week from the washing, standing in the same lane. She said it plainly, without any of the ornament I would have given it.[p]She said: I am not bright in the way you think. I am bright *here*.[p]I heard a countrywoman afraid of the coach road. I heard modesty, which I had by then decided was a symptom of the very quality I was measuring, so that her denial became evidence for me rather than against. If you wish to know the exact moment my whole science became [gloss:Unfalsifiable], it is there - in a lane, in February, in the mouth of the only person who ever told me the truth about it.[p]*She was not being humble. She was being accurate. She was telling me the location of the thing I claimed to study, and I wrote it down as charm.*
				Fifth: I want the good months on the record, and I will not have them read as prologue to anything.[p]We were happy in a plain way I had not previously believed was available to me. The parish was happy around us. The figures rose all that spring, and I told myself I was witnessing something and not causing it. She learned my subject well enough to catch me in it. She asked, once, in June, whether I would keep her name out of anything I published, and I said yes without hesitation, because at that moment I meant it entirely.[p]*A man who breaks a promise he never meant has committed one offence. A man who breaks one he did mean has committed two, and the second is against himself, and is the only one he is likely to grieve over. I have grieved over it a great deal. It has been a comfortable grief, and I recommend it to nobody.*
				Closing: She agreed to come in the spring. Her mother was three years dead, the cottage went to a cousin, and there was, everyone agreed, nothing to keep her.[p]I remember the phrase being used, kindly. I remember agreeing with it. I would give the rest of my life to have that afternoon back, so that I might ask what, precisely, everybody thought *keeping* meant.[p]She was quiet on the road. I put it down to the cold. By the second day she had begun asking me questions about the city - what she should read before we arrived, what she ought not to say - and I answered every one of them at length and with pleasure. I have thought since that a woman does not ask what she ought not to say unless she has understood that she is being brought somewhere as an exhibit.
			}

			Ch4: {
				Title: Ch. 4 | The House on Cardell Street
				Opening: The house was a good one. I record this because it was the first thing I said to her when we arrived, and it is the first thing I have said about it since. I have begun to notice how often a man describes a room by its quality when he cannot describe what happened in it.[p]Three floors, a garden the length of a cart, and a window at the back that took the western light for most of the afternoon. I had taken it in January on the strength of a [gloss:Stipend] I did not yet have. I showed her every room in turn and told her which would be hers. She asked where the water came from. I did not know; I remember laughing about it.[p]She found out within the week. She found out everything of that kind within the week, and never mentioned any of it to me again.
				Second: They came on Thursdays at first, then on Thursdays and Sundays, and by the second winter there was a list and I was keeping it.[p]I have the list still. Eleven names in the first year, forty-one in the second, and beside each one the date and the duration and a mark of my own devising for the strength of the impression received. I had thought this rigorous. Whitlock came four times. Vandermeer wrote from Leiden asking to be entered, and I entered him with a satisfaction I can still taste.[p]*I did not charge admission. I have been accused of it and it is not true, and I notice that I have spent thirty years correcting the accusation I could disprove.*
				Third: I published her name in the spring of the second year.[p]I had promised I would not. I want to set down the reasoning exactly as it stood in my head at the time, because I do not think it was villainous and I do not think it was rare, and a warning is no use to anybody if the man in it is a monster.[p]The paper would not stand without the name. An unnamed subject is an anecdote; a named one is a case. I told myself that the promise had been made to a woman in a lane who could not have understood what she was declining, and that she had since grown into a person who would understand, and that the grown person would consent - and then I did not ask the grown person, because asking would have introduced the possibility of no.[p]*Every step of that reasoning is sound except the one I did not take. This is generally how it goes: a chain of good sense with a hole where the question should be.*
				Excerpt: '''"Nobody here needs anything from me. They only want to see."'''
				AsideTitle: On what I did not measure
				AsideBody: My instrument recorded the disposition of a place toward itself: who was lent to, where quarrels stopped, which houses the sick were carried to. I ran it on Cardell Street for three years. The figures were flat, and I concluded that the city was too large to register. It did not occur to me until 1809 that I had built an instrument which could only read a person through the people around her, and had then removed her from all of them.
				Fourth: She was vain about her hands. I have said so already, and I say it again because it is the only measurement I took that turned out to mean anything.[p]In the parish they were a labourer's hands, and she was proud of them in the particular way of a woman who is good at something the world does not count. By the second summer in Cardell Street they were soft. I noticed. I noticed, and I was pleased. If you wish to know what kind of man I was in 1803, that is the whole of it in one observation: I saw the evidence of a life removed and read it as a life improved.[p]She had no washing to hang; we had a girl for that. She had no neighbours to lend to, nobody to be carried to her door, no market to walk to. Four hours a week became all of them, and I filled the hours with books, because books were what I had.
				Fifth: She began, in that second year, to speak well.[p]I recorded it as the great vindication of my project. Her sentences lengthened. She acquired the vocabulary of my discipline and used it with precision. The visitors remarked upon it. Vandermeer wrote that she had become *quite the most articulate instance in the literature*, and I copied the phrase into three letters.[p]*She had begun to speak like me. That is all it was. I had spent two years pouring myself into a person, and I stood back and found the vessel full and called the contents a discovery. She thought Locke evasive once, in a sentence I could not answer. By the third year she thought what I thought about Locke, and could say it at length, and I never noticed the moment the sentence I could not answer stopped being available to me.*
				Sixth: She asked to go home in the November of the third year. Once, and not again.[p]She was not unwell in any way a physician could name, though I had two of them to the house and they were both very sure of it. She said she wanted to see the lane. I said we would go in the spring, and I meant it, and there was a paper to finish, and then there was the second paper, and Vandermeer came in April with two men from the Society, and by the summer she had stopped asking and I recorded the cessation as *settling*.[p]I did not take her. That is the sentence. I have written this chapter four times looking for a longer one, and there is not a longer one. I did not take her, and there was no reason I did not, and no obstacle, and the road was three days.
				Closing: What she became in the last two years is what I built my reputation upon. I will describe it as accurately as I am able, because the accuracy is the point, and because nobody who came to the house would say I exaggerated.[p]She was luminous. Everyone said so. Every man on that list said so, and several wrote it down. She was calm, and gracious, and unfailingly the same. She received forty-one visitors and gave each of them the identical impression; the impressions can be laid side by side and do not differ by a hair. I presented this consistency as my strongest result.[p]It was consistency. That is exactly what it was. A thing which does not vary is not being observed under different conditions - it has simply stopped varying. She was fixed. She had become a portrait of herself, and portraits keep beautifully, and I hung mine where the western light would catch it in the afternoons, and invited men from three countries to come and look.[p]She was bright there. I have written that sentence a hundred times in this room, hoping the word *there* would consent to mean this house, and it will not, and it has not, and I no longer expect it to.
			}

			Ch5: {
				Title: Ch. 5 | TrelamiumIII
				Opening: I have called these last years a TrelamiumIII in letters to men who wrote to ask after my health, and I chose the word because it was pleasant, and because it allowed them to stop asking. A TrelamiumIII is a soft thing. A man in a TrelamiumIII is a man who will presently come out of one.[p]I looked the word up in October. I do not know why; I had used it forty times by then without curiosity, which is the condition I have been in for most of my life. It is from the old French, and the old French did not mean daydreaming. It meant delirium. It meant raving, and wandering, and a mind gone out of its own house and not returning.[p]So I have done it again. I took the pleasant sense of a word and built on it, exactly as I did at twenty-six, and this time the other sense has come and sat down opposite me and will not be corrected. I am writing this while I can still tell you which of the two I mean.
				Second: The forty pages are done. You may stop believing me.[p]I withdraw the Illuminance. I want to be precise about the grounds, because I am not withdrawing it for being false - I could not find it false, and if you have read the preceding chapters you will understand by now that this was never a virtue of the doctrine but its central defect. I withdraw it for being the wrong name for a true thing.[p]There *is* something. I was not deluded about that, and I have been informed by well-meaning correspondents that I was, and I find their kindness harder to bear than the accusation. There is something. I described its outline for thirty years with great care and considerable skill. What I got wrong was every single question of what it is, where it lives, whose it is, and which way round it faces.
				Third: I saw it in the November before last, at about four in the afternoon, in the room at the back that takes the western light.[p]She was sitting as she sat. I had come in with a letter in my hand and I looked up, and what was in the chair was a shape with light behind it, and I knew it immediately and intimately and in every particular, the way you know your own handwriting on a paper you do not remember writing.[p]I had been drawing that shape since I was twenty-six. I had drawn it from behind. I knew the contour of it better than any living man and I had never once seen the front, and the front was in my house, in the good chair, in the light I had chosen the house for.[p]I wrote in the second chapter that I did not find the Illuminance. I have never written anything truer and I did not know what I was writing. I did not find it.[p]It found her.
				Excerpt: '''"No diminution in the fifth year. The instance holds." - from my own paper, 1806'''
				AsideTitle: On the proper form of a retraction
				AsideBody: A term is withdrawn by letter to the society that received it, naming the paper, the page, and the nature of the error, with a request that the correction be read into the minutes at the next sitting. I have done this. It took eleven lines and was accepted without discussion. There is no form, in any literature I have been able to consult, for withdrawing a word from the person you put it on.
				Fourth: I have spent two years attempting to establish my own guilt, and I have failed, and I need you to understand that the failure is the worst thing that has ever happened to me.[p]The case for it is strong. I took her from the lane. I emptied her hours. I brought forty-one men to look at her and kept the list in my own hand. The case against it is also strong, and it was put to me by a physician who meant to be comforting: that she was already the brightest thing in eleven parishes before I ever walked into one, that the shape was in my figures before I knew her name, and that whatever came for her had chosen her while I was still three counties away and writing up somebody else's village.[p]I cannot settle it. I have the notebooks and the figures and thirty years of method and I cannot settle the only question I have ever needed to answer.[p]And I find - I set this down because a warning ought to be complete - I find that I want to be guilty. A guilty man is a cause. A cause is a thing with a hand upon a lever. I would take the monstrousness gladly if it came with the lever, and it does not, and it has not, and the mere possibility that I stood beside it and merely *watched* is a thing my mind puts down and picks up again forty times in a night.
				Fifth: Here is what it is, as nearly as I can put it, and it is the last useful sentence I expect to write.[p]It is not light. It comes off light; it cannot arise without light; it will not touch a dull man or a dull house or anything that has never wanted anything. But it is not light, and I called it light for thirty years and gave the world a word that says so. The false light. That is the name it should have had, and I am aware that the naming is the last of my vices and I am doing it anyway.[p]It grows under looking. That is the whole of the mechanism and I will not dress it up. To attend to it is to feed it; to record it is to feed it; to bring a second man to see it is to feed it twice. I published forty-one instances of a thing that swells when observed, and I gave the swelling as my proof, and it did not once occur to me that the list was not my evidence.[p]The list was my method.
				Sixth: She is upstairs. I want that understood, because my correspondents write as though there had been a funeral, and there has not been one, and there will not be one for a long while yet.[p]She is well. She eats. She answers when addressed and she answers graciously and she has answered in the same manner for six years. She is luminous - I use the word deliberately and for the last time, since every man who ever came to the house used it and not one of us was describing her.[p]I am not permitted to mourn. Mourning wants a loss and there has been no loss; there has been a *stopping*, and there is no rite for it, and no black to wear, and no morning on which anybody will tell me that it does get easier. She is not gone. She is here, entirely, unchangingly, in the good chair, in the western light, and I may sit with her every afternoon for the rest of my life and I will never once be able to say that I have lost her.
				Closing: My physician wishes me to record that I have been sleeping poorly and that some of what precedes may be attributed to it. I have recorded it. He is a kind man and he is wrong about which part.[p]I no longer go into the back room in the afternoons. I go in the mornings, before the light comes round, because in the mornings the chair is only a chair and she is only a woman who has not yet been looked at that day, and for an hour or so I can hold on to the idea that the whole of it was arithmetic and I made an error in the arithmetic.[p]She told me once that the lane was public and she could hardly stop me. I called it the kindest thing anyone had ever said to me and I did not know why for thirty years, and I know now, sitting here, and it is very simple. She was telling me the place was open. She was telling me that a thing may be visited without being carried off.[p]*I have written to the cousin. The road is three days and the washing lines will still be up in April, and I mean to take her in the spring - I have meant it before and I mean it now, and I am aware of how that sentence has gone previously and I do not care. We will go in the spring. She will be bright there again. And I will stand at the end of the lane with my hands empty and I will not write anything down.*
			}
		}

